aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cub2NhZmV6aW5oby5jb20vMjAyNi0xMS0wMy9xdWF0cm8tY2lkYWRlcy1kby1wYXJhbmEtc29sdGFtLW1vc3F1aXRvcy1jb20tYmFjdGVyaWEtcXVlLWJsb3F1ZWlhLWEtZGVuZ3VlLw==,DISMISS,これはデング熱等の対策（ワクチン的介入に相当するWolbachia蚊放出）に関する公衆衛生施策の記事であり、新規の異常な感染症発生や感染拡大を報告するものではない。,Zika; Chikungunya; Dengue,Londrina; Ponta Grossa; Paraná; Cambé; Federative Republic of Brazil,Four cities in Paraná release mosquitoes carrying bacteria that block dengue.,Quatro cidades do Paraná soltam mosquitos com bactéria que bloqueia a dengue,2026-11-04T01:52:47+00:00,https://www.ocafezinho.com/2026-11-03/quatro-cidades-do-parana-soltam-mosquitos-com-bacteria-que-bloqueia-a-dengue/,PORTUGUESE,humans; vectors,"Four Paraná cities—Cascavel, Ponta Grossa, Cambé, and Londrina—began releasing Wolbachia-infected Aedes aegypti mosquitoes in October to block transmission of dengue, Zika, and chikungunya. The 26-week operation is led by Fiocruz with the Ministry of Health and implemented by Wolbito do Brasil alongside local authorities. Wolbachia, a bacterium common in many insects but absent in Aedes aegypti, inhibits replication of dengue, Zika, and chikungunya viruses inside the mosquito, reducing transmission as the infected population becomes predominant. Developed by the World Mosquito Program at Monash University, this approach has shown dengue reductions in other Brazilian cities. Cascavel aims to reach 57% of its population in 17 neighborhoods; Londrina will release over a million Wolbitos across 47 neighborhoods; Ponta Grossa and Cambé began in October with 26-week release plans. Brazil recorded more than 6 million probable dengue cases in 2024; four serotypes exist, with reinfection raising risk. Outcomes will compare treated and untreated neighborhoods.",No,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,No,No,0,No,Yes,No,記事はデング熱・ジカ熱・チクングニア熱の予防的対策（Wolbachia感染蚊の放出プログラム）に関するものであり、新規発生や流行の急拡大を示す内容ではないためDISMISSと判定した。,
aHR0cHM6Ly9zdW1zZWwudHJpYnVubmV3cy5jb20vc3Vtc2VsLzEwMzQ0MDUvcGVta2FiLW9rdS10aW11ci1yYWloLWF0bS1hd2FyZC0yMDI2LXBvcnRhYmxlLXgtcmF5LXBlcmt1YXQtZGV0ZWtzaS1kaW5pLXRiYw==,DISMISS,地方自治体の保健プログラム表彰に関する行政広報であり、アウトブレイクや症例急増等の公衆衛生上の異常事態は報告されていない。,Tuberculosis; Malaria; HIV,Jawa Barat; Sumatera Selatan; Bandung,"East Ogan Komering Ulu Regency Government Wins ATM Award 2026, Portable X-Ray Strengthens Early TB Detection","Pemkab OKU Timur Raih ATM Award 2026, Portable X-Ray Perkuat Deteksi Dini TBC",2026-11-04T01:59:24+00:00,https://sumsel.tribunnews.com/sumsel/1034405/pemkab-oku-timur-raih-atm-award-2026-portable-x-ray-perkuat-deteksi-dini-tbc,INDONESIAN,humans,"East Ogan Komering Ulu Regency (OKU Timur) in South Sumatra, Indonesia, wins the AIDS-Tuberculosis-Malaria (ATM) Award 2026 for strengthening the local health system. The award recognizes sustained efforts against HIV/AIDS, tuberculosis (TB), and malaria, accepted by OKU Timur Regent Ir H Lanosin MT MM at the National Workshop of ADINKES in Bandung on 3 November 2026. A concrete step is the August 2026 deployment of a Portable X-Ray unit donated by the Ministry of Health to accelerate TB screening and early case finding. HIV/AIDS services are enhanced through Perawatan, Dukungan, dan Pengobatan (PDP) facilities supporting testing, patient mentoring, and treatment. The Bupati emphasizes the prize is a mandate to expand primary health, prevention, and equitable access via multi-sector collaboration. No TB case counts are reported.",No,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,記事は結核・HIV・マラリア対策の表彰および保健機材導入に関する地方行政ニュースであり、症例数の報告や異常な増加は記載されていない。,
aHR0cHM6Ly9zdWNraG9lZG9pc29uZy52bi9tZS1iaS1jdW0tYS1sYW0tdGhlLW5hby1kZS1raG9uZy1sYXktYmVuaC1jaG8tdHJlLXNvLXNpbmgtMTY5MjYxMTAyMjIzNjI0OTQ3Lmh0bQ==,DISMISS,一般的な育児・健康情報記事であり、公衆衛生上の異常事態やアウトブレイクを示す内容ではない。,Influenza A; Influenza,,A mother with influenza A: how can she prevent transmitting the disease to newborns?,"Mẹ bị cúm A, làm thế nào để không lây bệnh cho trẻ sơ sinh?",2026-11-04T02:13:00+00:00,https://suckhoedoisong.vn/me-bi-cum-a-lam-the-nao-de-khong-lay-benh-cho-tre-so-sinh-169261102223624947.htm,VIETNAMESE,humans,"Influenza A in a breastfeeding mother does not transmit via breast milk; mothers may continue nursing if well, with precautions to prevent respiratory spread. Flu A spreads through respiratory droplets, so close contact with the infant poses exposure risk. Pumping milk is acceptable; when possible, a healthy family member should feed the baby using expressed milk. Key measures: thorough handwashing before touching the infant; wearing a well-fitted mask during feeding or near the baby; avoid kissing the infant’s face or hands while symptomatic; ensure a ventilated room and clean pumping equipment. If possible, another caregiver handles diapering and soothing. Infants under 6 months cannot be vaccinated; household members should receive annual influenza vaccination. Seek early medical care for mother (two weeks postpartum higher risk) or infant if warning signs appear; oseltamivir may be used with low transfer into breast milk.",No,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,季節性インフルエンザに関する育児アドバイス記事で、アウトブレイクや異常事態の報告ではない。,
