aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cua2JjLmNvLmtlL2tlbXJpLWpvaW5zLWdsb2JhbC1kZW5ndWUtcmVzZWFyY2gtYWxsaWFuY2Uv,DISMISS,ケニアの研究機関が国際デング研究アライアンスに参加したという政策・研究協力に関するニュースであり、対象国における急性の公衆衛生上の異常事態を報じるものではない。,Dengue,Africa; Republic of the Sudan; Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia; Malaysia; Republic of Kenya; Republic of Uganda; Republic of Senegal,,KEMRI joins global Dengue research alliance,2026-11-04T07:50:25+00:00,https://www.kbc.co.ke/kemri-joins-global-dengue-research-alliance/,ENGLISH,humans; vectors; environment,"Kenya becomes the first African country to join the Dengue Alliance, established in 2022, to advance safe, effective dengue treatments and strengthen research, surveillance, diagnostics and clinical management. Kenya's membership will expand collaboration and ensure African patients are considered in future clinical studies. WHO data show about 10,000 dengue cases in Kenya from January to August 2026, up from 4,300 in 2025 and 1,400 in 2024, with all four dengue virus serotypes detected since the first outbreak in 1982. Transmission has extended beyond coastal areas into northern and western Kenya and near borders with Uganda, South Sudan and Ethiopia. While vaccines and vector-control have advanced, no specific dengue treatment exists. A DNDi–Serum Institute of India candidate is slated for Phase III in early 2027 (Brazil, Thailand, Malaysia). DNDi-led studies in Senegal, Ghana and DRC underscore underdiagnosis and underreporting, guiding new medicines development; KEMRI will contribute arboviral research and climate-health expertise.",No,No,No,No,0,No,不明,No,No,No,0,No,No,No,記事の主題はケニアにおけるデング熱症例増加と研究アライアンス参加であり、ケニアは対象国リストに含まれない。Ghana、DRC、Thailand、Brazilは研究協力や将来の臨床試験実施地として言及されているのみで、これらの国での実際の発生事例を報じるものではないためverified_locationsから除外した。,
